OBJDUMP(1)                  User Commands                OBJDUMP(1)

NAME
    objdump - display information from object files

SYNOPSIS
    objdump [-afhpxtTrRsw] [-j section]... file...

DESCRIPTION
    objdump prints headers, symbols, relocations and section contents of
    ELF64 files of x86_64 and aarch64. The input is an executable, a
    shared object, a relocatable object or a System V archive of such
    objects. For an archive, objdump prints the line "In archive NAME:"
    and the output of each member.

    The output equals the output of GNU objdump (version 2.47) for the
    implemented options. At least one of the options -a, -f, -h, -p, -x,
    -t, -T, -r, -R or -s is required.

    Disassembly is not available. The options -d, -D and -S print the
    message "disassembly is not available" and exit with status 1. A later
    release adds the disassembler.

OPTIONS
    -a, --archive-headers   print the archive header of each member. For a
                            plain file, print the file name
    -f, --file-headers      print the architecture, the BFD flags and the
                            start address
    -h, --section-headers   print the table of sections
    --headers
    -p, --private-headers   print the program headers, the dynamic section
                            and the version definitions and references
    -x, --all-headers       select -a -f -h -p -r -t
    -t, --syms              print the symbol table
    -T, --dynamic-syms      print the dynamic symbol table
    -r, --reloc             print the relocations of each section
    -R, --dynamic-reloc     print the dynamic relocations
    -s, --full-contents     print the contents of the sections in
                            hexadecimal and as characters
    -j, --section=NAME      restrict -h, -s, -r, -t and -T to the section
                            NAME. The option is repeatable
    -w, --wide              print the section flags on the line of the
                            section and widen the name column
    -H, --help              print a usage message
    -v, --version           print the version of the program

EXAMPLES
    objdump -h prog
    objdump -x libjson.so
    objdump -s -j .rodata prog
    objdump -t -j .text libedit.a

SEE ALSO
    readelf(1), nm(1), size(1), strings(1), ar(1)
